{
  "rationale": "The binding constraints here are the burst latency and 12-bit accuracy, not raw throughput. A 1 MHz tick dumps 32 requests at once and all must return within 0.44 us, i.e. the unit must sustain ~32/0.44 ≈ 73 results/us during the burst (well above the 32 msps throughput floor) plus pipeline latency. That immediately rules out `iterative`: at 1 result per N+3 cycles it would need Fmax ≈ 73·(N+3) ≥ 500 MHz, impossible on Artix-7. `pipelined` and `pipelined_m` both issue 1 result/cycle, so they only need Fmax ≈ (32+N+2)/0.44 ≈ 100–125 MHz, which is the only realistic way to satisfy sys_p99_batch_us; `pipelined_m` is the area-lever (fewer pipeline registers → lower luts_plus_ffs, at the cost of Fmax), so it is the likely area-optimal family and deserves a large share. `unrolled_k` gets a small share only to test whether k=8 can reach the required issue rate. Accuracy needs roughly W≥14 (output LSB 2^-(W-2)) and N≥13 (residual ≈2^-N), so ranges are centred there but kept wide enough to map the area-vs-accuracy front up to ~18 bits for hypervolume coverage. Rounding and both guard fields are left open because they buy accuracy cheaply and shift the front.",
  "families": [
    {
      "why": "1 result/cycle gives the highest Fmax of the feasible families, so it has the best chance of clearing sys_p99_batch_us with margin (important because the L1 bound is optimistic and L2 re-screens). It registers every stage, so it is FF-heavy and anchors the low-latency / high-area end of the front.",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 12, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 10, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]}
      ],
      "family": "pipelined",
      "budget_share": 0.45
    },
    {
      "why": "Same 1 result/cycle throughput as `pipelined` but registers only every m stages, cutting FFs and therefore luts_plus_ffs — the primary objective. The risk is a longer combinational path lowering Fmax below what the burst bound needs, so m is kept small (2–6) to preserve enough clock speed while still saving registers. This is the most likely source of the area-optimal, selection-winning design.",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 12, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 10, "high": 20, "choices": null},
